          ":question/id": "4d7555ca-db2c-52bc-b443-fc91fca13d67",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-131631",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x-3y+6z &=9 \\\\ -x+4z &=4 \\\\ x+y-2z &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 & 6 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & \u2194{R}_{3} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-4z &=-4 \\\\ y+2z &=1 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4z-4 \\\\ y &=1-2z \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4z-4 \\\\ 1-2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
